(LST2-74 — the fizzbuzz example: `but if` clauses are checked in order and
 the FIRST match wins, which is why 6 and 12 print "fizzbuzz" and not
 "fizz". The manual's own two print examples, verbatim.
 Also covers: LST2-75 (the even/odd example), LST2-78 (clauses checked in
 order), LST2-82 (first match wins), LST2-83 (multiple clauses chain).
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox LST2-74.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   1
   fizz
   buzz
   fizz
   5
   fizzbuzz
   7
   fizz
   buzz
   fizz
   11
   fizzbuzz
   13
   fizz
   buzz
)
print each number from 1 to 15,
    but if the number modulo 6 is equal to 0 print "fizzbuzz",
    but if the number modulo 2 is equal to 0 print "fizz",
    but if the number modulo 3 is equal to 0 print "buzz".
